{"@context":{"lean":"lean:","block":"block:","imports":{"@type":"@id"}},"@graph":[{"@id":"lean:module:QOU.Information.ReebTuringClassical","@type":"LeanModule","name":"QOU.Information.ReebTuringClassical","imports":["lean:module:Init","lean:module:Mathlib.Computability.Halting"],"declarations":1,"chunks":1},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Information.ReebTuringClassical","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Information.ReebTuringClassical","kind":"theorem","line":64,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Information/ReebTuringClassical.lean#L64-L82","doc":"**Classical Reeb Turing-completeness (Cardona–Miranda–Peralta-Salas–Presas).**\n\nThere is a Reeb / steady-Euler flow on a closed 3-manifold whose first-return\ndynamics is Turing-universal; consequently its reachability predicate is\n**not decidable** — no computable procedure decides it. We take the\ncomputational shadow: there exists a predicate on the countable type of\nprogram codes that is **not** `ComputablePred` — the halting problem.\n\nThis is an **external theorem** (published proof), realised here as the\nproved `q = 1` anchor of `conj:quantum-computational-universality`. Unlike\nthe prior encoding it is a genuine, **sorry-free** theorem: the\nundecidability is the Mathlib halting problem `ComputablePred.halting_problem`\n— and, crucially, it quantifies non-decidability over **computable**\nprocedures (`ComputablePred`), which the old `∃ dec : ℕ → Bool` shape failed\nto do (that shape is provably false; see the module docstring). ","formalizes":[{"@id":"block:prop:reeb-turing-completeness-classical","label":"prop:reeb-turing-completeness-classical","md":"quantum-observable-universe/information-theory/reeb-turing-completeness-classical.md"}],"signatureChunk":0}]}